In this lesson we'll look at how to plot points on the Cartesian plane. Picture two number lines crossing at right angles. The horizontal one is called the x-axis, with positive numbers to the right and negative numbers to the left. The vertical one is the y-axis, with positives going up and negatives going down. Where they meet is the origin, which we write as the ordered pair zero, zero. Together the two axes split the plane into four quadrants.

Now, every point on the grid gets a name, and it's called an ordered pair, written x then y. To plot the point three, four, you start at the origin, move three units to the right along the x-axis, then move four units up. And here's the important part, order matters, so three, four and four, three land in completely different spots. Always go across first, then up.

When you've got a table of values, just treat each row as an ordered pair, plot each point, and then connect them with a straight line or a smooth curve to reveal the rule. Now let's see it in action.